                <h2>Common Reasons for Account Suspension &amp; Initial Diagnosis</h2>
<p>Google Ads account suspension is rarely random. Before preparing appeal materials, you must identify the actual cause of the suspension.</p>
<p><strong>Quick Diagnosis Steps:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Log into Google Ads and navigate to "Policy Management"</li>
<li>Check for red warning indicators</li>
<li>Click on specific violations to view official policy explanations</li>
<li>Review recent advertising behavior changes</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Top 5 Common Suspension Reasons:</strong><br />
- Circumventing review systems (frequent ad modifications or pause/enable cycles)<br />
- Promoting content violating Google Ads policies<br />
- Inaccessible landing pages or non-compliant content<br />
- Intellectual property complaints<br />
- Payment issues or abnormal account information</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Practical Tip:</strong> Many advertisers rush to appeal after receiving suspension notices without reading the violation details carefully. Spend 10-15 minutes reading the complete policy documentation first—this is your guide for material preparation.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Appeal Materials Checklist &amp; Preparation Steps</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Gather Basic Information Materials</h3>
<p>Regardless of violation type, prepare the following in advance:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Material Type</th>
<th>Specific Content</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Account Info</td>
<td>Google Ads Customer ID, registration email</td>
<td>Found in account settings</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Business License</td>
<td>Clear color scan</td>
<td>Must match account entity</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>ID Document</td>
<td>Front/back of ID or passport</td>
<td>Required in some cases</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Website Related</td>
<td>Domain certificate, hosting provider info</td>
<td>Proof of site ownership</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Step 2: Prepare Specialized Materials Based on Violation Type</h3>
<p><strong>Scenario A: Website/Landing Page Violations</strong><br />
- Website rectification plan (describe modifications made or planned)<br />
- Product/service compliance certificates<br />
- Privacy policy page screenshot<br />
- SSL certificate (ensure HTTPS is working)</p>
<p><strong>Scenario B: Intellectual Property Complaints</strong><br />
- Trademark registration or copyright certificates<br />
- Brand authorization documents (if agent)<br />
- Communication records with complainant or settlement agreements<br />
- Product procurement invoices or authorization chain documents</p>
<p><strong>Scenario C: Abnormal Account Information</strong><br />
- Address proof (utility bills, bank statements)<br />
- Clear photos of credit card or payment method (sensitive info can be redacted)<br />
- Business description documents from registration</p>
<h3>Step 3: Draft the Appeal Email</h3>
<p>The appeal email is the key document determining success.</p>
<p><strong>Email Structure Template:</strong></p>
<pre><code>Subject: Account [Customer ID] Appeal Request - [Brief violation type]

Body Points:
1. Brief self-introduction and business background
2. Acknowledge possible violations (don't argue)
3. List rectification measures already taken
4. Explain future compliant advertising plans
5. Express sincere rectification attitude
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Note:</strong> Use English for emails when possible—response times are often faster. Avoid emotional language, and never threaten or pressure.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Appeal Submission &amp; Follow-up</h2>
<h3>Submission Channels</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Direct Backend Appeal:</strong> Google Ads → Help → "Contact Us" → Select account suspension option → Complete appeal form</li>
<li><strong>Email Appeal:</strong> policy-support@google.com (note: responses not guaranteed)</li>
<li><strong>Google Ads Official Twitter/X Account:</strong> Can try in urgent situations</li>
</ol>
<h3>Post-Submission Follow-up Checklist</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Within 24 hours after submission, check email for Google's auto-reply</li>
<li>[ ] Wait 3-5 business days; don't submit duplicate appeals</li>
<li>[ ] Monitor account status changes and email notifications</li>
<li>[ ] If no response received, proactively query via backend</li>
<li>[ ] For re-appeals, add new materials and explain differences from first attempt</li>
</ul>
<h3>Strategies When Appeal Is Rejected</h3>
<p>First rejection is common. Don't give up easily.</p>
<p><strong>Key Points for Second Appeal:</strong><br />
- Include first appeal case reference number<br />
- Emphasize additional rectifications completed since first appeal<br />
- Add new supporting materials<br />
- If falsely suspended, prepare more detailed business explanation documents</p>
<hr />
<h2>Daily Operations: Preventing Account Suspension</h2>
<ul>
<li>Regularly check Policy Management page for new violation warnings</li>
<li>Self-check policies before any ad copy or landing page adjustments</li>
<li>Maintain account activity; avoid sudden restart after long inactivity</li>
<li>Keep account registration materials secure; ensure information consistency</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Summary:</strong> The core of account appeals is diagnose first, prepare second, follow up third. When preparing materials, more is better than less. After submission, stay patient and avoid repeated follow-ups. Hope this checklist helps you recover your account quickly and continue business growth.</p>
